1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,179
Some emails are long, and the one thing they ask of you
is buried in the middle.

2
00:00:07,229 --> 00:00:14,376
If you cannot skim, because of dyslexia, low vision, or
because you cannot rely on sound,

3
00:00:14,426 --> 00:00:19,570
finding that request means reading everything again and
again.

4
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:25,403
Jeri, by team FullStuckEngineer, does not just
summarise.

5
00:00:25,453 --> 00:00:33,880
It finds what the email asks of you, shows the evidence,
checks the dates, and leaves the decision to you.

6
00:00:33,930 --> 00:00:38,490
This is the live app, using a fictional example email.

7
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:44,658
We open an interview invitation from Northbridge, a
fictional company.

8
00:00:44,708 --> 00:00:47,136
Gemini reads the email once.

9
00:00:47,186 --> 00:00:51,884
While it works, the page shows where each answer will
appear.

10
00:00:51,934 --> 00:00:55,050
This wait is real, and shortened here.

11
00:00:56,414 --> 00:01:00,429
Here is the result. What the email is. Your ask:

12
00:01:00,479 --> 00:01:05,945
confirm the interview, or propose two other times, using
their form.

13
00:01:05,995 --> 00:01:11,244
And below it, the exact sentences from the email that
support it.

14
00:01:11,996 --> 00:01:14,570
Now the part a summary would miss.

15
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:17,569
Our code, not the AI, checks every date.

16
00:01:17,619 --> 00:01:22,943
The email says Wednesday the twelfth of November, but
that date is a Thursday.

17
00:01:22,993 --> 00:01:29,941
The deadline is written as Thursday the thirteenth,
which is a Friday, and it falls after the interview.

18
00:01:29,991 --> 00:01:32,878
Jeri does not guess which day is right.

19
00:01:32,928 --> 00:01:39,626
It shows what the email says, what the check found, and
links each warning to the original sentence.

20
00:01:41,881 --> 00:01:47,611
There are three ways to take this in, all from the same
analysis.

21
00:01:47,661 --> 00:01:53,311
Listen reads short spoken beats aloud. It never plays by
itself.

22
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:00,550
[App voice] You need to reply to this email or fill out the form.

23
00:02:00,675 --> 00:02:06,345
[App voice] Aiko Sato invited you to a second-round interview for
the Northbridge internship.

24
00:02:06,474 --> 00:02:09,431
[App voice] You are asked to confirm your interview attendance or
propose two other times using the form by Thursday,

25
00:02:09,481 --> 00:02:16,586
[App voice] Warning. The email says Wednesday, 12 November 2026, at
16:00 JST.

26
00:02:16,636 --> 00:02:21,711
[App voice] Our check found that this date is a Thursday.

27
00:02:21,822 --> 00:02:25,097
[App voice] Warning. The email says Thursday, 13 November 2026,
17:00 JST.

28
00:02:25,801 --> 00:02:27,800
Switch to Captions.

29
00:02:27,850 --> 00:02:34,022
The audio stops, and the same sentence appears as text,
in the same place.

30
00:02:34,072 --> 00:02:36,071
No sound is needed.

31
00:02:39,104 --> 00:02:44,121
Easy Read shows large, plain cards, one topic each.

32
00:02:44,171 --> 00:02:47,643
Every 'not' is kept. For example:

33
00:02:47,693 --> 00:02:53,054
you do not need your residence card for the video call.

34
00:02:54,105 --> 00:02:59,971
At the bottom, the Draft Dock holds a reply that asks
which day they mean,

35
00:03:00,021 --> 00:03:03,938
instead of confirming a date that may be wrong.

36
00:03:03,988 --> 00:03:08,122
Approve records your decision: approved, not sent.

37
00:03:08,172 --> 00:03:10,359
Jeri never sends email.

38
00:03:10,409 --> 00:03:14,615
And if you edit the draft, the approval is cleared.

39
00:03:18,364 --> 00:03:24,055
Under the hood, Gemini analyses the email once, into a
structured result.

40
00:03:24,105 --> 00:03:27,670
Our code verifies the quotes and the dates.

41
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:31,143
Gemini text to speech provides the voice.

42
00:03:31,193 --> 00:03:36,176
Results are saved, so switching modes never calls the AI
again.

43
00:03:36,226 --> 00:03:44,114
Gmail sign-in is built, but not connected in this demo,
so it uses a built-in inbox of fictional emails.

44
00:03:45,764 --> 00:03:51,719
Jeri finds the ask, keeps the meaning, and lets you
choose the next step.

45
00:03:51,769 --> 00:03:54,314
Jeri, by FullStuckEngineer.
